# Golden set for the `nilus` graph (3,833 nodes / 7,007 edges; Jira + Bitbucket + Notion).
# Run: uv run python -m scripts.evaluate_retrieval eval/nilus_golden.jsonl --graph nilus --k 8
#
# Purpose: this set exists to answer ONE question about ontology auto-adoption —
#   "after expanding the rulebook, is anything lost and do answers stay the same?"
# So it is a REGRESSION set first and an improvement set second.
#
#   kind=control (30) — pass today. MUST still pass after adoption. This is the
#                       "no information is lost" half. A drop here blocks the change.
#   kind=target  (10) — about the 473 facts currently refused as relation_not_allowed.
#                       Expected to be weak today and to improve after adoption.
#
# `kind` and `note` are ignored by the scorer (extra fields are tolerated).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- BASELINE
# Recorded 2026-09-16, before any ontology adoption. Full run in
# eval/nilus_baseline_2026-09-16.json.
#
#   control  31/31 passing   MRR=0.6876   recall@8=1.0000
#   target    0/10 passing   MRR=0.0000   recall@8=0.0000
#   overall                  MRR=0.5199   recall@8=0.7561   precision@8=0.1600
#
# Control recall is exactly 1.0 by construction: every control case's expected
# node is in the top 8 today. That is what makes this usable as a regression
# gate -- ANY drop below 1.0 after adoption is unambiguous and blocks the change.
# Target is exactly 0.0 for the same reason in reverse: any rise is attributable
# to adoption and nothing else.
#
# Measured through `graph.chat.retrieve`, which is the composition the product
# uses (structured resolver + ranker + name matcher + time window). An earlier
# run scored the ranker alone and reported 28/31 -- three false failures.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- CONTROL: Jira
